    {
      "kind": "page",
      "number": 3,
      "title": "Multi threaded process",
      "images": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Multi threaded process",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "A thread is like another copy of a process that executes PC1 independently",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Threads shares the same address space (code, heap)",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "PC2",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Each thread has separate PC",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Each thread may run over different part of the program",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Each thread has separate stack for independent function calls",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "SP2",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "SP1",
          "level": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "page",
      "number": 4,
      "title": "Process vs. threads",
      "images": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Process vs. threads",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Parent P forks a child C",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "P and C do not share any memory",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Need complicated IPC mechanisms to communicate",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Extra copies of code, data in memory",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Parent P executes two threads T1 and T2",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "T1 and T2 share parts of the address space",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Global variables can be used for communication",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Smaller memory footprint",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Threads are like separate processes, except they share the same address space",
          "level": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "page",
      "number": 5,
      "title": "Why threads?",
      "images": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why threads?",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Parallelism: a single process can effectively utilize multiple CPU cores",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Understand the difference between concurrency and parallelism",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Concurrency: running multiple threads/processes at the same time, even on single CPU core, by interleaving their executions",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Parallelism: running multiple threads/processes in parallel over different CPU cores",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Even if no parallelism, concurrency of threads ensures effective use of CPU when one of the threads blocks (e.g., for I/O)",
          "level": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "page",
      "number": 6,
      "title": "Scheduling threads",
      "images": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Scheduling threads",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "OS schedules threads that are ready to run independently, much like processes",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "The context of a thread (PC, registers) is saved into/restored from thread control block (TCB)",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Every PCB has one or more linked TCBs",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Threads that are scheduled independently by kernel are called kernel threads",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "E.g., Linux pthreads are kernel threads",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "In contrast, some libraries provide user-level threads",
          "level": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "User program sees multiple threads",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Library multiplexes larger number of user threads over a smaller number of kernel threads",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "Low overhead of switching between user threads (no expensive context switch)",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "list_item",
          "text": "But multiple user threads cannot run in parallel",
          "level": 1
        }
      ]
    },
